  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Colorado has received $273M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. Obligations fell 98.1% year over year. Award activity includes 845 awards with an average award size of $324K. The Department of Homeland Security is the top buyer in the state with $89.1M, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $61.9M and the Department of the Interior with $36.5M. ARDENT GROUP, LLC tops the vendor list with $44.8M, and THE GEO GROUP, INC. follows with $39.9M; LOCKHEED MARTIN CORP is third with $30M. The largest industry by obligations is Pharmacy Benefit Management and Other Third Party Administration of Insurance and Pension Funds at $44.8M, and Security Guards and Patrol Services is next at $36.8M. Historical context shows fiscal-year obligations were $14.3B in FY22, $15.4B in FY23, $15.6B in FY24, $16.5B in FY25, and $14.5B in FY26. Note. All figures are contract obligations and FY2027 totals are partial (fiscal year to date).",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why are FY2027 obligations so low compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Colorado obligations fell 98.1% year over year. Fiscal-year totals were $14.5B in FY26 and $273M in FY27, showing a large drop in reported obligations for the current fiscal year to date. The FY2027 total is a partial, fiscal year to date figure, which affects comparability with full-year totals in prior years."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate FY2027 buying in Colorado?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security is the largest buyer with $89.1M, followed by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $61.9M and the Department of the Interior with $36.5M. Pharmacy Benefit Management and Other Third Party Administration of Insurance and Pension Funds is the largest industry at $44.8M, and Security Guards and Patrol Services is second at $36.8M."
      }
    ]
  },
